WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.766
Moving away from native Java serialization
helps,

2
00:00:02.846 --> 00:00:04.954
but it is not the end of serialization
security.

3
00:00:05.034 --> 00:00:07.195
JSON, XML, and YAML are common Java data
formats,

4
00:00:07.275 --> 00:00:10.068
and they are often safer when used as
constrained data formats.

5
00:00:10.148 --> 00:00:12.731
They can still create risk when parser
settings are broad,

6
00:00:12.811 --> 00:00:14.444
when untrusted data controls types,

7
00:00:14.524 --> 00:00:16.843
when external resources are allowed
unexpectedly,

8
00:00:16.923 --> 00:00:20.454
or when application code binds directly
into sensitive domain objects without

9
00:00:20.534 --> 00:00:21.114
validation.

10
00:00:21.194 --> 00:00:23.566
Polymorphic type handling deserves careful
review.

11
00:00:23.646 --> 00:00:27.335
Some libraries and framework features can
choose implementation classes based on

12
00:00:27.415 --> 00:00:28.627
type metadata in the input.

13
00:00:28.707 --> 00:00:31.816
Default typing, class-name-based binding,
broad subtype handling,

14
00:00:31.896 --> 00:00:35.217
and custom deserializers can be dangerous
when driven by untrusted input.

15
00:00:35.297 --> 00:00:37.141
If the application expects a request DTO,

16
00:00:37.221 --> 00:00:40.963
the caller should not be able to decide
which arbitrary Java class gets instantiated

17
00:00:41.043 --> 00:00:41.676
to satisfy it.

18
00:00:41.756 --> 00:00:44.707
XML parsing should consider external
entities, schema behavior,

19
00:00:44.787 --> 00:00:48.054
entity expansion, external resource
access, and parser configuration.

20
00:00:48.134 --> 00:00:51.665
YAML parsers can construct richer
structures or types depending on library and

21
00:00:51.745 --> 00:00:52.483
configuration.

22
00:00:52.563 --> 00:00:55.515
JSON parsers can allow unknown fields,
deeply nested structures,

23
00:00:55.595 --> 00:00:58.230
very large arrays, or coercions that
surprise developers.

24
00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:02.104
Framework defaults should be reviewed
rather than assumed safe because defaults may

25
00:01:02.184 --> 00:01:04.134
vary by version, library, and integration.

26
00:01:04.214 --> 00:01:05.637
Prefer explicit DTOs, schemas,

27
00:01:05.717 --> 00:01:09.354
and allowlisted fields over arbitrary maps
or type-driven object construction.

28
00:01:09.434 --> 00:01:12.754
Unknown fields, unexpected types,
oversized values, deeply nested data,

29
00:01:12.834 --> 00:01:15.522
and unsupported versions should be handled
intentionally.

30
00:01:15.602 --> 00:01:17.710
The lesson is not that JSON, XML, or YAML
are bad.

31
00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.479
The lesson is that constrained parsing and
explicit mapping are what make ordinary

32
00:01:21.559 --> 00:01:23.140
formats safer at trust boundaries.
